The function should take the head of a linked list as input and return the root of the binary tree formed using the linked list values. The linked list is guaranteed to be non-empty and each node contains a single integer value.

For example, given the linked list: 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5, the resulting binary tree should look like:
```
     1
    / \
   2   3
  / \
 4   5
```

### Solution:
```java
public BinaryTreeNode linkedListToBinaryTree(ListNode head) {
    if (head == null) {
        return null;
    }

    BinaryTreeNode root = new BinaryTreeNode(head.data);
    Queue<BinaryTreeNode> queue = new LinkedList<>();
    queue.add(root);
    head = head.next;

    while (head != null) {
        BinaryTreeNode parent = queue.poll();
        BinaryTreeNode leftChild = new BinaryTreeNode(head.data);
        parent.left = leftChild;
        queue.add(leftChild);
        head = head.next;

        if (head != null) {
            BinaryTreeNode rightChild = new BinaryTreeNode(head.data);
            parent.right = rightChild;
            queue.add(rightChild);
            head = head.next;
        }
    }

    return root;
}
```

The provided solution implements the function `linkedListToBinaryTree` which iterates through the linked list to construct the binary tree level by level using a queue. It ensures that each node in the binary tree is linked to its corresponding children based on the linked list values.

